1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,238
Watch a pulse of light, just a few wave cycles long,

2
00:00:04,278 --> 00:00:07,275
race along a silicon wire toward a tiny ring.

3
00:00:07,345 --> 00:00:10,292
Some of it hops into the ring, circles around and around,

4
00:00:10,332 --> 00:00:13,265
and leaks back out a little on every lap.

5
00:00:13,335 --> 00:00:15,439
Every model so far was an approximation.

6
00:00:15,479 --> 00:00:20,304
This is Maxwell's equations themselves, solved on a grid. How?

7
00:00:20,374 --> 00:00:22,249
It's called finite-difference time-domain,

8
00:00:22,289 --> 00:00:25,510
FDTD, and it rests on two ideas:

9
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:29,482
leapfrogging fields, and a border made of complex numbers.

10
00:00:30,302 --> 00:00:32,591
In two dimensions, the electric field

11
00:00:32,631 --> 00:00:34,154
E points straight out of the screen,

12
00:00:34,194 --> 00:00:37,651
and the magnetic field H lies flat in the plane.

13
00:00:37,721 --> 00:00:39,718
Maxwell's equations then say two things:

14
00:00:39,758 --> 00:00:42,846
a changing H is driven by the curl of E,

15
00:00:42,886 --> 00:00:45,963
and a changing E by the curl of H.

16
00:00:46,033 --> 00:00:50,127
The curl measures swirl: how much a field circulates around each point.

17
00:00:50,197 --> 00:00:54,292
Watch a bump of E. Its slopes make H start to circulate around it.

18
00:00:54,362 --> 00:00:58,571
That swirling H lowers E in the middle and raises it around the edge.

19
00:00:58,611 --> 00:01:02,639
The bump spreads into a ring: a wave that pushes itself along.

20
00:01:02,709 --> 00:01:04,599
These rules contain only derivatives,

21
00:01:04,639 --> 00:01:08,874
which compare each point with its neighbours. Everything is local.

22
00:01:09,694 --> 00:01:12,354
On a computer, sample the fields on a grid,

23
00:01:12,394 --> 00:01:15,507
and replace each derivative by a difference of neighbours.

24
00:01:15,577 --> 00:01:17,664
But that difference, divided by the spacing,

25
00:01:17,704 --> 00:01:19,848
gives the slope best at the midpoint

26
00:01:19,888 --> 00:01:22,405
between the samples, not at either end.

27
00:01:22,475 --> 00:01:26,478
So in 1966, Kane Yee put H exactly at those midpoints:

28
00:01:26,518 --> 00:01:28,689
half a cell away from E.

29
00:01:28,759 --> 00:01:31,980
Time gets the same trick: E lives on whole time steps,

30
00:01:32,020 --> 00:01:34,524
and H on the half steps in between.

31
00:01:34,594 --> 00:01:38,384
The algorithm is a leapfrog. Use the curl of E to update H,

32
00:01:38,424 --> 00:01:41,693
then the curl of H to update E, and repeat.

33
00:01:41,763 --> 00:01:44,034
Here it is in 1D, with real numbers:

34
00:01:44,074 --> 00:01:48,872
each new value uses only its two neighbours, yet the pulse marches on.

35
00:01:48,942 --> 00:01:52,072
Matter is just one number per cell, the permittivity ε.

36
00:01:52,112 --> 00:01:55,822
Any shape you can paint on the grid, you can simulate.

37
00:01:56,642 --> 00:02:01,290
The grid has two rules of its own. First, a speed limit: each step,

38
00:02:01,330 --> 00:02:05,156
news travels only to neighbouring cells, so it spreads in a diamond.

39
00:02:05,226 --> 00:02:10,211
Light spreads in a circle, which must fit inside the diamond:

40
00:02:10,251 --> 00:02:14,736
in 2D, c Δt ≤ Δx/√2. This is the Courant limit.

41
00:02:14,806 --> 00:02:18,477
Break it by just six percent, and the computed field explodes.

42
00:02:19,147 --> 00:02:23,049
Second, the grid distorts waves. Recall the k-circle:

43
00:02:23,089 --> 00:02:25,904
in free space, all directions share one wavelength.

44
00:02:25,974 --> 00:02:29,273
On the grid, the circle bulges, most along the axes:

45
00:02:29,313 --> 00:02:33,645
short waves travel too slowly, slowest along the grid lines.

46
00:02:33,715 --> 00:02:36,240
A pulse becomes a squarish ring, trailed by ripples.

47
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:40,555
The fix: ten to twenty cells per wavelength, or more.

48
00:02:41,375 --> 00:02:44,297
Now the hardest part: the grid must end somewhere,

49
00:02:44,337 --> 00:02:46,291
but real light just keeps going.

50
00:02:46,361 --> 00:02:49,441
If the grid simply stops, its edge acts as a mirror,

51
00:02:49,481 --> 00:02:51,566
and the echo ruins the answer.

52
00:02:51,636 --> 00:02:53,379
So add a layer that absorbs?

53
00:02:53,419 --> 00:02:57,302
A sudden change of material reflects too, right at its edge.

54
00:02:57,372 --> 00:02:59,727
A gentler layer must be thick and expensive.

55
00:02:59,767 --> 00:03:02,690
We need an absorber that light cannot see.

56
00:03:04,310 --> 00:03:09,361
The trick, found in 1994 by Bérenger, and by Chew and Weedon,

57
00:03:09,401 --> 00:03:12,047
is to change not the material, but the coordinates.

58
00:03:12,117 --> 00:03:15,817
Inside the border, let the position x pick up an

59
00:03:15,857 --> 00:03:21,281
imaginary part that grows with depth: x + (i/ω)∫σ dx.

60
00:03:21,351 --> 00:03:27,270
A wave e^(ikx) at this complex position keeps its wavelength,

61
00:03:27,310 --> 00:03:32,969
but gains a factor e^(−(k/ω)∫σ dx): it dies away.

62
00:03:33,039 --> 00:03:36,089
These are the same Maxwell equations in stretched coordinates,

63
00:03:36,129 --> 00:03:41,479
so the border matches perfectly: no echo, at any angle or frequency.

64
00:03:41,549 --> 00:03:45,767
This is the perfectly matched layer, or PML. Side by side:

65
00:03:45,807 --> 00:03:50,513
the wall echoes, the lossy layer echoes, the PML stays silent.

66
00:03:50,583 --> 00:03:54,357
On a grid, a tiny echo remains, so σ ramps up smoothly.

67
00:03:54,397 --> 00:03:58,440
Grazing and evanescent waves are the hardest to absorb.

68
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:02,817
In code, the whole method is four lines inside a time loop.

69
00:04:02,887 --> 00:04:06,725
Update H from the curl of E. Update E from the curl of H.

70
00:04:06,765 --> 00:04:09,390
Absorb in the PML. Add the source.

71
00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:12,390
Send in one short pulse, record what comes back,

72
00:04:12,430 --> 00:04:14,542
and a Fourier transform in time gives

73
00:04:14,582 --> 00:04:16,783
every wavelength from a single run.

74
00:04:16,853 --> 00:04:19,447
For a Bragg mirror, a stack of alternating layers,

75
00:04:19,487 --> 00:04:23,591
the whole reflection spectrum lands right on the exact answer.

76
00:04:23,661 --> 00:04:26,197
The price: every cell is updated at every step.

77
00:04:26,237 --> 00:04:28,741
A small 2D run takes seconds;

78
00:04:28,781 --> 00:04:33,102
a large 3D device, minutes to hours on GPUs.

79
00:04:33,172 --> 00:04:36,951
And it sees what simpler models miss: on this fine glass relief,

80
00:04:36,991 --> 00:04:42,267
the thin-element model sends 96% of the light one way. FDTD,

81
00:04:42,307 --> 00:04:46,668
with shadows and light trapped in the ridges, finds 57%.

82
00:04:47,488 --> 00:04:51,756
FDTD isn't exact either: it converges as cells shrink,

83
00:04:51,796 --> 00:04:56,157
curves become staircases, and the PML adds a small error.

84
00:04:56,227 --> 00:04:59,027
But the core idea is simple. E updates H,

85
00:04:59,067 --> 00:05:01,842
and H updates E, one neighbour at a time.

86
00:05:01,912 --> 00:05:05,679
A layer of complex-stretched space absorbs whatever leaves.

87
00:05:05,749 --> 00:05:09,782
Now we can simulate almost anything. What we really want is the reverse:

88
00:05:09,822 --> 00:05:12,994
say what the light should do, and get the structure.

